  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜てしまう (te-shimau accidental/regretful)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Attach しまう (shimau) to a verb's te-form to express that something has been done completely, often with a feeling of regret or that it happened unintentionally. Think of it as 'ended up doing' or 'unfortunately did.'\n\nExample from this song: 知ってしまった (shitte shimatta) = 'ended up knowing' — from 知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知って (shitte) + しまった (shimatta, past of shimau)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べてしまった (tabete shimatta, 'ate it all up / accidentally ate it') — from 食べる (taberu, 'to eat')\n• 忘れてしまった (wasurete shimatta, 'ended up forgetting') — from 忘れる (wasureru, 'to forget')\n• 壊してしまった (kowashite shimatta, 'broke it by accident') — from 壊す (kowasu, 'to break')\n\nNote: In casual speech, てしまう contracts to ちゃう (chau) and てしまった to ちゃった (chatta). Example: 食べちゃった (tabechatta)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "知る (shiru, 'to know') → 知って (shitte) → 知ってしまった (shitte shimatta, 'ended up knowing')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜たびに (tabi ni - every time)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Attach たびに (tabi ni) after a verb in dictionary form or a noun + の to express 'every time' or 'whenever.' It describes a pattern that repeats.\n\nExample from this song: 強がるたびに (tsuyogaru tabi ni) = 'every time I act tough' — from 強がる (tsuyogaru, 'to act tough/bluff')\n\nMore examples:\n• 会うたびに (au tabi ni, 'every time we meet') — from 会う (au, 'to meet')\n• 思い出すたびに (omoidasu tabi ni, 'every time I remember') — from 思い出す (omoidasu, 'to recall')\n• 旅のたびに (tabi no tabi ni, 'every trip') — noun + の + たびに\n\nNote: たび (tabi) literally means 'time/occasion.' The pattern always uses the plain/dictionary form of the verb before たびに."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "強がる (tsuyogaru, 'to act tough') + たびに (tabi ni, 'every time') → 強がるたびに (tsuyogaru tabi ni, 'every time I act tough')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜なくても (nakutemo - even without)",
